    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nThese are orthogonal to **data parallelism (DP)**, where full model replicas process different requests. In production inference stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron-based servers), TP and EP are typically combined with DP replicas behind a load balancer. This note focuses on the mechanics of TP and EP within a single forward pass.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block. The two main matmuls are handled differently:\n\n**Attention projections (column-then-row parallelism):**\n\n- The QKV projection `W_qkv \u2208 R^{d\u00d73d}` is **column-parallel**: each of the `T` tensor-parallel ranks holds `W_qkv^{(i)} \u2208 R^{d \u00d7 3d/T}`. Each rank computes its slice of the output: `Y_i = X \u00b7 W_qkv^{(i)}`, giving it `3d/T` columns of the projected activations.\n- Because attention heads are independent, each rank owns a contiguous block of `H/T` heads and can run attention locally with no communication.\n- The output projection `W_o \u2208 R^{d\u00d7d}` is **row-parallel**: each rank holds `W_o^{(",
